# Competitor feature coverage parity (hard rule)

Operator directive (Phil, 2026-10-01), verbatim:

> **Hard rule: make sure that every feature our competitors cover, we must
> cover the same. If we don't have the feature, marked it as "Planned" or
> the way we show IGA and Certificate Management.**

## The rule

> **Every feature a major Competitor publicly covers appears on our
> capability surfaces: the capabilities page, the industry terms page,
> requirements checklists, comparison grids, and the matching solution
> page. Each row carries exactly one status. A feature we ship is
> "Available now". A feature we ship in part is "Available now, wider
> scope planned" with one plain sentence on the current scope. A feature
> we do not ship is "Planned". A Competitor feature is never left off the
> list because we lack it.**

Buyers score against a requirements list built from Competitors'
features. A missing row reads as "not supported". A row marked Planned
reads as a roadmap commitment, and the rows marked Available now show
everything we do.

## The two reference patterns

Copy these shapes exactly.

**Partial coverage - the Identity Governance and Administration (IGA)
pattern** (`capabilities.md`):

```text
| Identity Governance and Administration (IGA) | Account lifecycle and periodic access review for kit accounts. Governance across other applications is planned. | Available now, wider scope planned |
```

State what ships today, then name the planned extension in one sentence.

**No coverage yet - the Certificate lifecycle management pattern**
(`capabilities.md`, `industry-terms.md`):

```text
| Certificate lifecycle management | Discovery, renewal, and governance of certificates across certificate authorities. | Planned |
```

Use the industry name for the feature and a plain one-line description.
Do not add an apology or an explanation of why the feature is missing.

## Required agent behavior

1. When building or revising a capability surface, list the features the
 major Competitors publish for that category, using the vendor set in
 [`competitor-presentation-parity.mdc`](competitor-presentation-parity.mdc).
 Use their datasheets, feature pages, and published requirements lists.
2. For each feature, look up AIC evidence in the source pages and product
 docs. Assign Available now, Available now with wider scope planned, or
 Planned. A row with no evidence is Planned, never Available now.
3. Name each feature with the term Competitors use
 ([`competitor-vocabulary-commercial-only.mdc`](competitor-vocabulary-commercial-only.mdc)).
 Expand acronyms on first use.
4. Add a feature in every place it belongs. A row on a checklist also
 belongs on the capabilities page and the industry terms page.
5. Keep the rows current. When a Planned feature ships, change its status
 in the same change set as the product change, on every surface.
6. Never name a Competitor in website copy
 ([`no-competitor-names-in-website.mdc`](no-competitor-names-in-website.mdc)).
 Record which vendor publishes each feature in `docs/dev`, the
 competitive notes, or the commit body.
7. Mirror this file to `core-assets/.cursor/rules/` in the same change
 set ([`auto-commit-push.mdc`](auto-commit-push.mdc)).

## Forbidden

- Omitting a Competitor feature from a capability surface because AIC
 does not ship it.
- Marking a feature Available now without source evidence.
- Status words other than Available now, Available now with wider scope
 planned, Planned, and Organization.
- Dates, release quarters, or durations on Planned rows
 ([`us-terminology-word-choice.mdc`](us-terminology-word-choice.mdc)).
- Explanations, apologies, or caveats attached to a Planned row.
- Inventing a feature name instead of using the Competitors' term.

